---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseStudyHeader from "../../components/CaseStudyHeader.astro";
import CaseStudyTOC from "../../components/CaseStudyTOC.astro";
import BarChart from "../../components/charts/BarChart.astro";
import ArchDiagram from "../../components/charts/ArchDiagram.astro";

const tocItems = [
  { id: "business", label: "The business case" },
  { id: "use-case", label: "The use case" },
  { id: "work", label: "The work" },
  { id: "orchestration", label: "Orchestration" },
  { id: "deterministic", label: "Making it deterministic" },
  { id: "results", label: "Results" },
  { id: "next", label: "What's next" },
  { id: "stack", label: "Stack" },
];

const stats = [
  { value: "1 chat", label: "company → live demo" },
  { value: "66/66", label: "demo leads verified" },
  { value: "<30 min", label: "full demo seed" },
  { value: "Dry run", label: "default for every edit" },
];

const iconStep = [
  { label: "Model picks icons one by one", value: "~8 min · 52 turns", fill: 1 },
  { label: "One batched tool call", value: "1 call", fill: 0.04, accent: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Daedalus · Paritosh Tripathi"
  description="Daedalus — an agentic pipe that turns a prospect's description into a configured, populated CRM pipeline for a sales demo, with LLMs for judgment and deterministic code for every write."
>
  <CaseStudyHeader
    slug="daedalus"
    title="Daedalus"
    blurb="An agentic pipe that turns a description of a prospect's business into a configured CRM pipeline, populated with a believable sales team and leads at every stage — ready to demo, and editable live by conversation."
    stats={stats}
    ownership="Built on Sharpsell's in-house agent framework"
  />

  <CaseStudyTOC items={tocItems} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 prose-invert">
    <h2 id="business">The business case</h2>
    <p>
      Sharpsell sells a lead-management product that every client configures for their own sales motion. Winning a deal starts with a demo, and a demo only lands if the prospect sees <strong>their</strong> process: their stages, their activities, their team, with leads already moving through it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Building that by hand took <strong>days</strong>. Someone authored a large pipeline manifest, then spent hours creating users, a reporting hierarchy and dozens of leads, walking each one through real actions so it sat in the right stage with a believable history. Every prospect paid that cost before the first meeting, and any change requested in the room meant going back to the admin UI.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal: compress setup to <strong>one conversation</strong>, so pre-sales can show a tailored, working pipeline the same day — and change it live while the prospect watches.
    </p>

    <h2 id="use-case">The use case</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Describe the company.</strong> "A regional insurer whose agents sell policies door to door."</li>
      <li><strong>Research.</strong> An agent works out the sales process and writes a handoff: pipelines, stages, activities, the org chart, personas, and a demo story — how many leads each rep owns, at which stage, and a few featured leads with a scripted history.</li>
      <li><strong>Build and import.</strong> An agent turns the handoff into a pipeline manifest and imports it into the client's tenant.</li>
      <li><strong>Seed.</strong> On a yes, an agent creates the team and walks every lead through real actions until it sits where the story says, then audits the result.</li>
      <li><strong>Edit live.</strong> "Add a site-visit stage after the demo." The change is previewed in plain language, applied on approval, and existing leads are protected.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="work">The work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Demo-data seeder</strong> — a ~29,700-line Python library with 529 tests that compiles the demo story into an exact seeding plan, executes it against the live LMS and Core APIs, and judges the outcome</li>
      <li><strong>Seed agent</strong> — wraps the seeder in the agent framework: stateless per request, with its workspace snapshotted to session storage after every write so a turn can resume on another worker</li>
      <li><strong>Edit agent</strong> — 15 tools for reading and changing a live pipeline, with readable previews and code-enforced approval</li>
      <li><strong>Agent edit API</strong> — 2 read and 12 write endpoints on the LMS backend for stages, transitions, activities and actions</li>
      <li><strong>Orchestration</strong> — routing between the four agents, the hand-offs between them, and eval graders that check tool calls, not just answers</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="orchestration">Orchestration</h2>
    <ArchDiagram
      title="Daedalus · orchestrator → research → create → seed / edit"
      caption="Orange arrows are hand-offs taken by code, not by a model's routing decision. The orchestrator's LLM only routes free-form requests: questions, edits, more users or leads."
    >
      <svg viewBox="0 0 920 330" class="w-full h-auto" role="img" aria-label="Daedalus orchestration">
        <style>
          .dd-node { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
          .dd-node-accent { fill: #1a1a1a; stroke: #ff8c00; }
          .dd-store { fill: #121212; stroke: rgba(80,227,194,0.35); }
          .dd-label { fill: #ededed; font: 500 12px "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace; }
          .dd-sub { fill: rgba(237,237,237,0.55); font: 400 10px "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace; }
          .dd-arrow { stroke: rgba(255,255,255,0.25); stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }
          .dd-arrow-accent { stroke: #ff8c00; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }
          .dd-judge { stroke: #50e3c2; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; stroke-dasharray: 4 4; }
        </style>
        <defs>
          <marker id="dd-ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)"></path>
          </marker>
          <marker id="dd-ah-saf" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#ff8c00"></path>
          </marker>
          <marker id="dd-ah-mint" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#50e3c2"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <rect x="20" y="40" width="170" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node" />
        <text x="105" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Orchestrator</text>
        <text x="105" y="84" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">routes free-form asks</text>

        <rect x="230" y="40" width="170" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node" />
        <text x="315" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Research agent</text>
        <text x="315" y="84" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">company → handoff</text>

        <rect x="440" y="40" width="190" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node" />
        <text x="535" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Create agent</text>
        <text x="535" y="84" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">manifest → import</text>

        <rect x="670" y="40" width="200" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node-accent" />
        <text x="770" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Seed agent</text>
        <text x="770" y="84" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">compile → approve → run</text>

        <rect x="230" y="170" width="170" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node" />
        <text x="315" y="198" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Edit agent</text>
        <text x="315" y="214" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">preview before apply</text>

        <rect x="440" y="170" width="190" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-node-accent" />
        <text x="535" y="198" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Agent edit API</text>
        <text x="535" y="214" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">dry run · advisory lock</text>

        <rect x="670" y="170" width="200" height="60" rx="10" class="dd-store" />
        <text x="770" y="198" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Live CRM tenant</text>
        <text x="770" y="214" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">pipelines · users · leads</text>

        <rect x="670" y="260" width="200" height="50" rx="10" class="dd-node" />
        <text x="770" y="282" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-label">Judge</text>
        <text x="770" y="298" text-anchor="middle" class="dd-sub">GET-only audit + score</text>

        <path class="dd-arrow" marker-end="url(#dd-ah)" d="M190 70 L230 70" />
        <path class="dd-arrow-accent" marker-end="url(#dd-ah-saf)" d="M400 70 L440 70" />
        <path class="dd-arrow-accent" marker-end="url(#dd-ah-saf)" d="M630 70 L670 70" />
        <path class="dd-arrow" marker-end="url(#dd-ah)" d="M105 100 L105 200 L230 200" />
        <path class="dd-arrow-accent" marker-end="url(#dd-ah-saf)" d="M400 200 L440 200" />
        <path class="dd-arrow-accent" marker-end="url(#dd-ah-saf)" d="M630 200 L670 200" />
        <path class="dd-arrow" marker-end="url(#dd-ah)" d="M770 100 L770 170" />
        <path class="dd-judge" marker-end="url(#dd-ah-mint)" d="M770 230 L770 260" />
      </svg>
    </ArchDiagram>
    <p>
      Each specialist is a sub-agent on one LangGraph graph. When a sub-agent finishes, an <code>on_sub_agent_return</code> hook decides what happens next — in code:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Research → create.</strong> When research registers a handoff file for the session, the graph jumps straight into the create agent with a fixed instruction. No model turn decides it.</li>
      <li><strong>Create → seed.</strong> When the import succeeds and a pipeline id is registered, the graph asks one button question — create demo data now? — and only on yes jumps into the seed agent.</li>
      <li><strong>Seed → release.</strong> A seed turn that ran the judge releases the chat even if the model forgot to hand back, so the next request is routed instead of refused.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The flags behind those hops live in session storage and are <em>taken</em> exactly once. The create → seed hop peeks at its flag before asking and only takes it after the answer, because resuming an interrupt re-runs the node from the top — taking it first would spend the hop on the question itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The session is the security boundary. Agents never accept a pipeline id, storage path or file name from the model; they resolve them from the session's own state, so a chat can only touch the pipeline it created.
    </p>

    <h2 id="deterministic">Making it deterministic</h2>
    <p>
      The rule throughout: <strong>models make judgments, code makes writes.</strong> The LLM researches, routes and talks; everything that touches a customer tenant goes through a compiled, reviewed path.
    </p>

    <h3>The seed plan is compiled, not generated</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The seeder reads the manifest's own stage rules and simulates every action against them. To place a lead, a breadth-first search explores action paths to the target stage, expanding steps in sorted order — the same story and manifest always give the same path</li>
      <li>Rule expressions that can't be replayed are refused: <code>$random</code> and <code>$millis</code> are rejected outright, and <code>$now()</code> is replaced with a fixed clock</li>
      <li>The finished plan is sealed with a <strong>content hash as its id</strong>. Nothing writes until the signed-in user approves that exact id, and approval only succeeds if the plan's counts match the story</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Execution is replayable</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Every write intent and outcome goes to a durable ledger. Resume never repeats a write — tested with a crash at every ledger boundary, a torn ledger tail, and an HTTP 500 that still returned an id</li>
      <li>An unknown outcome stays failed rather than being guessed into success, and demo identities are namespaced (<code>SYN-demo_&lt;uuid&gt;</code>) so two demos in one tenant never collide</li>
      <li>The judge then audits the live tenant with GET requests only and scores every featured lead's history against the story</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Edits have one write path and a code-enforced preview</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Every edit is an update import of a modified copy of the stored manifest, through the existing importer — the same validation, diff and publish as a normal import</li>
      <li>Writes default to a dry run. An apply is refused in code unless the user's previous turn was shown its preview; the prompt says the same, but doesn't have to be obeyed for the guard to hold</li>
      <li>A per-pipeline Postgres advisory lock serialises edits, and the importer refuses to remove a stage that still holds active leads</li>
      <li>Backend diffs full of UUIDs are rewritten into lines like <code>add transition 'Demo fixed' → 'Meeting done'</code>, so the person approving actually knows what they're approving</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Evals check behaviour, not wording</h3>
    <p>
      Custom graders assert on tool calls — for example, that a write tool was previewed with <code>dry_run=True</code> but never applied — across routing, stage, action and full-flow cases.
    </p>

    <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prospect description to a configured, populated demo pipeline in <strong>one conversation</strong>, instead of days of setup and data entry</li>
      <li>A real seed, independently verified with GET requests: <strong>66/66</strong> story leads, 17 synthetic users, reporting links intact, zero duplicate phones</li>
      <li>Profiling real runs removed the biggest costs: icon selection went from ~8 minutes of a 27-minute seed to one batched call, and users are created four at a time per org level</li>
      <li>600+ tests across the seeder and agents</li>
    </ul>
    <BarChart
      title="Icon selection in a 27-minute seed"
      rows={iconStep}
      caption="107 searches and 18 picks over 52 model turns, replaced by one batched call with a one-by-one fallback."
    />

    <h2 id="next">What's next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Run the test suites in CI</li>
      <li>Undo for applied edits, from the stored baseline each edit already starts from</li>
      <li>Judge scores tracked over time as an eval dashboard</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Python, LangGraph / LangChain deep agents on Sharpsell's agent framework</li>
      <li>JSONata rule simulation for stage placement</li>
      <li>PostgreSQL advisory locks in the LMS backend</li>
      <li>Cloud Tasks workers with session-storage snapshots</li>
      <li>pytest plus custom eval graders</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
